explanation:
A **Promise** is an object representing the eventual success or failure of an asynchronous operation. Instead of passing callbacks into a function, you attach them to the promise it returns. `.then()` itself returns a *new* promise, which is why calls chain.

An `async` function always returns a Promise: it resolves with whatever the function returns, or rejects with whatever it throws. Inside it, `await promise` suspends the function until that promise settles, letting asynchronous code read like ordinary top-to-bottom code:

```js
const wait = (ms, value) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(value), ms));
async function main() {
  const a = await wait(20, "first");   // pauses here until resolved
  const b = await wait(10, "second");  // starts only after `a` is done
  return `${a} then ${b}`;
}
main().then((text) => console.log(text)); // first then second
```

Awaiting one call after another runs them **in sequence**. When the operations are independent, start them all and wait together with `Promise.all([...])`, which runs them concurrently and gives you an array of their results. If any of them rejects, `Promise.all` rejects immediately; `Promise.allSettled` instead waits until every operation is complete.

steps:
Run it:
```js
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
const wait = (ms, value) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(value), ms));
const getUser = (id) => wait(50, { id, name: "Ana" });
const getScore = (id) => wait(50, 42);
async function loadCard(id) {
  const [user, score] = await Promise.all([getUser(id), getScore(id)]);
  return `${user.name}: ${score}`;
}
const started = Date.now();
const card = await loadCard(1);
const elapsed = Date.now() - started;
assert.equal(card, "Ana: 42");
assert.ok(elapsed < 90, `expected concurrent loading, took ${elapsed} ms`);
assert.ok(loadCard(1) instanceof Promise);
console.log(card, `${elapsed} ms`);
```
